<title>A graphical approach to finding equilibria of discrete dynamical systems</title>

<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Video 2</ref> | <ref>Transcripts</ref> </p>
<description>
By plotting the updating function and the diagonal, one can read off the equilibria from their intersections of the two graphs.
</description>



<p>One way to determine the 
<term><ref to="doenet:83xTXp7HL7ZhCob6Z6UoEM">equilibria</ref></term>
 of a 
<ref to="doenet:7C1w6bKbkKghbh9wUtwzxF">discrete dynamical system</ref>
 is to 
<ref to="doenet:7FmiE88UGp1HTdKmpQwzo2">determine the equation the equilibrium must satisfy</ref>
 and then solve that equation.</p>

<p>Here, we introduce a way of finding the equilibria from the graph of the updating function.  If we have a dynamical system in 
<term><ref to="doenet:82YaRL8CieueYqLgxMpxye">function iteration form</ref></term>
,
<md>
  <mrow>x_{n+1}\amp = f(x_n)</mrow>
  <mrow>x_0\amp = x_0,</mrow>
</md>
then, we can find the equilibria simply by plot the graph of <m>f</m>.  If <m>f</m> intersects the diagonal line where <m>x_{n+1}=x_n</m>, then we know the input of <m>f</m> was the same as its output.  If the updating function <m>f</m> holds a particular number fixed, then that number is an equilibrium.</p>


<section name="vid_section"><title>Video 1</title>


<p>The first video introduces the graphical approach of finding equilibria by looking at how we can solve equations graphically.</p>

