<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Which of the following best describes the meaning of the function <m>f(H_t)</m> shown in the graph below, and in the video?</p>
  <answer>
    <choice>The function <m>f</m> represents how the state variable changes as a function of time</choice>
    <choice credit="1">The function <m>f</m> represents the evolution rule of our dynamical system</choice>
    <choice>The function <m>f</m> is an expression of the given discrete dynamical system in <term><ref to="doenet:832kteFVLDpZJ9tARy27gK">difference form</ref></term></choice>
  </answer>


<feedback condition="$_choice1">Take another look at the horizontal axis on the graph, is it <em>time</em> that is changing; is it <em>t</em> that is an input to the function <m>f</m>? What is the input? You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 1</ref> in the video.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$_choice2">Excellent! The function <m>f</m> describes how we can <em>evolve</em> the dynamical system from one time step, <m>t</m>, to the next time step at <m>t+1</m>.  In other words, it is the function that changes <m>H_t</m> into <m>H_{t+1}</m>.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$_choice3">If you take another look at the evolution rule we are given in this example, <m>H_{t+1} = f(H_t)</m>, you can see that it is actually written in <term><ref to="doenet:82YaRL8CieueYqLgxMpxye">function iteration form</ref></term> form, not <term><ref to="doenet:832kteFVLDpZJ9tARy27gK">difference form</ref></term>. You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 1</ref> in the video.</feedback>

  <graph xmin="-1" ymin="-1" xmax="5" ymax="5">
    <xLabel><m>H_t</m></xLabel>
    <yLabel><m>H_{t+1}</m></yLabel>
    <function fixed="true" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="right">x^2
      <label><m>f(H_t)</m></label>
    </function>
    <line fixed="true" styleNumber="2" applyStyleToLabel="true">y=x
      <label><m>H_{t+1} = H_t</m></label>
    </line>
    <point fixed="true" styleNumber="2">(0,0)
      <label><m>(0,0)</m></label>
    </point>
    <point fixed="true" styleNumber="2">(1,1)
      <label><m>(1,1)</m></label>
    </point>
  </graph>

</exercise>
  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />  
<p>Refer to the example in the video. Which of the following is <alert><em>not</em></alert> true about the points where the graph of <m>H_{t+1}=f(H_t)</m> (i.e. the $_function1.styleDescriptionWithNoun) and the graph of <m>H_{t+1} = H_t</m> (i.e. the $_line1.styleDescriptionWithNoun) intersect?</p>
  <answer name="vid1Review1">
    <choice>these intersection points represent equilbrium solutions to the given dynamical system</choice>
    <choice>these points occur at locations where the function <m>f</m> does not change the value of its inputs, <m>H_t</m>.</choice>
    <choice>at these intersection points, it is always possible to determine the value of <m>H_{t+1}</m> from the value of <m>H_t</m> alone.</choice>
    <choice credit="1">these intersection points occur only where the value of the state variable is zero.</choice>
    <choice>at all intersection points <m>(H_t, H_{t+1})</m>, the value of the first coordinate, <m>H_t</m>, is equal to the value of the second coordinate, <m>H_{t+1}</m>.
    </choice>
  </answer>

<feedback condition="$_choice4">This is actually a true statement - the intersection points <em>do</em> represent equilibrium solutions to the dynamical system. You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> in the video.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$_choice5">This is actually a true statement - at equilbrium points the function <m>f(H_t)</m> leaves the value of the state variable unchanged. You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> in the video.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$_choice6">This is actually a true statement - at any equilibrium point, if you know the value of the input <m>H_t</m> (the value on the horizontal axis), then you also know the value of the output, <m>H_{t+1}</m> - they are the same! You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> in the video.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$_choice7">Correct! While there is indeed an equilibrium solution at the location where the value of the state variable, <m>H_t</m> is zero, there is also an equilibrium solution where <m>H_t = 1</m>. This is the one statement that is <em>not</em> true.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$_choice8">This is actually a true statement - all equilibrium points have the same format: <m>(E, E)</m>. You can find a clue to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> in the video.</feedback>
</exercise>

</problem>
